label start:
    # Prologue - Home Again
    scene black
    with fade

    centered "PROLOGUE"
    centered "Home Again"

    "Eight years."

    "That's how long it's been since you last lived in this little town."

    "You were 16 when you left."

    "Your parents had spent years working overseas, while your grandparents raised you here."

    "When your parents finally settled down, they brought you to live with them."

    "You remember being excited to see them."

    "You also remember crying the night before your flight."

    "You never got to graduate with your friends."

    "And now, at 24, you've come home again."

    "The streets look almost exactly as you remember."

    "The little convenience store at the corner is still there."

    "So is the bus stop where you used to wait after school."

    "You wonder how much everything has changed."

    "And how much has stayed the same."

    "But there's one thing you know is different."

    "Grandma isn't waiting at the gate anymore."

    "You tighten your grip on your suitcase."

    p "..."

    p "I'm home."

    scene black
    with dissolve

    "The front door opens before you can knock."

    g "There you are!"

    g "I was beginning to think you'd gotten lost."

    p "Grandpa, I grew up here."

    g "Eight years is a long time. Maybe you've forgotten the way."

    p "I haven't forgotten everything, you know."

    "He laughs."

    "Some things just never change."

    g "Come in. You must be hungry."

    "You step inside."

    "The house smells faintly of tea and old wood."

    "A pair of slippers is waiting by the entrance."

    "The same pair Grandma always insisted you wear."

    g "I've made something to eat."

    p "You cooked?"

    g "What's that supposed to mean?"

    p "Nothing! I'm just surprised."

    g "Your grandmother taught me a thing or two."

    "He says it casually."

    "But for a moment, neither of you speaks."

    p "..."

    p "I still can't believe she's gone."

    "Grandpa looks at you."

    g "Neither can I, sometimes."

    g "Yesterday, I nearly called out to ask where she'd put the tea leaves."

    "He gives a small laugh."

    g "Then I remembered."

    "You lower your eyes."

    p "I wish I'd come back more often."

    "Grandpa shakes his head."

    g "Your grandmother knew you were busy."

    g "And she was always happy whenever you called."

    g "You know what she used to say?"

    p "What?"

    g "That our little girl was all grown up now."

    "You smile faintly."

    p "She still called me little?"

    g "Of course."

    g "You could turn 80 and she'd still call you that."

    "You both laugh."

    "For a moment, the house feels a little less quiet."

    g "Anyway, go put your suitcase away."

    g "I'll call you when dinner's ready."

    g "Your room's just how you left it."

    scene black
    with fade

    centered "THE BEDROOM"

    "The door creaks when you push it open."

    "You stop at the doorway."

    "Your old desk is still beside the window."

    "Your bookshelf is still filled with textbooks and novels."

    "Even the little marks you made on the wall are still there."

    p "No way..."

    "It's almost exactly the same."

    "The room seems smaller than you remember."

    "Or maybe you've just grown."

    "You place your suitcase beside the bed."

    "A few cardboard boxes sit beneath your desk."

    "Some contain things you left behind at sixteen."

    "Others are filled with belongings you'd sent back when you moved apartments a few years ago."

    "You kneel and pull one of them closer."

    "Inside are old exercise books, photographs, and little things you'd forgotten you owned."

    p "I really kept all this stuff?"

    "You pick up an old notebook."

    "There are doodles in the margins."

    "Some are yours."

    "Some aren't."

    "A familiar drawing catches your eye."

    "A terribly drawn cat."

    p "..."

    p "Pfft."

    p "He really never could draw."

    "You turn the page."

    "Someone has written beside an unfinished math question."

    "{i}Stop daydreaming and finish your homework.{/i}"

    "You smile."

    "You remember the boy who wrote it."

    "The one who used to wait outside your grandparents' gate every morning."

    "The one who knew exactly how to annoy you."

    "Your best friend since you were kids."

    "You wonder how he's doing now."

    "When you first moved away, the two of you used to message all the time."

    "You told him about your new school."

    "He told you about everything happening back home."

    "Then there were exams."

    "New friends."

    "Different schedules."

    "Messages that took longer and longer to answer."

    "And eventually..."

    "You try to remember the last time you spoke."

    "You can't."

    p "...It's really been that long, huh?"

    "You carefully place the notebook aside."

    "That's when you notice something underneath it."

    "A small wooden picture frame."

    "You lift it from the box."

    "Five teenagers are smiling at the camera."

    "One of them is making a ridiculous face."

    "Another has their eyes closed."

    "And there you are."

    "Standing beside him."

    "Your closest friend since childhood."

    "You remember this photograph."

    "It was taken just before you left town."

    "He'd given you the frame as a farewell gift."

    "You'd promised to keep it."

    "And you did."

    "For years, it sat on the desk in your bedroom overseas."

    "Eventually, it found its way into a box when you moved apartments."

    "You trace a finger across the dusty glass."

    "The people in the photograph are all sixteen."

    "But you're twenty-four now."

    "Somewhere out there, they must be too."

    p "I wonder how everyone's doing."

    "You turn the frame over to wipe the dust from its back."

    "Something shifts beneath your fingers."

    p "Hm?"

    "The wooden backing has come slightly loose."

    "You try to press it back into place."

    "But as you lift one of the little metal clips, something slips out."

    "A folded piece of paper."

    p "...What's this?"

    "You pick it up."

    "The paper has yellowed slightly with age."

    "There's handwriting on the front."

    "Messy, familiar handwriting."

    "You unfold it carefully."

    jump hidden_letter

label hidden_letter:

    scene black
    with dissolve

    "{i}Hey, idiot.{/i}"

    "You laugh under your breath."

    p "Of course you'd start with that."

    "{i}I don't know when you'll find this. Maybe tomorrow, maybe a few years from now. Or maybe you won't find it at all.{/i}"

    "{i}Honestly, I don't even know why I'm writing this.{/i}"

    "{i}There's a lot I wanted to tell you before you left, but every time I tried, I couldn't get the words out.{/i}"

    "{i}I guess I was scared.{/i}"

    "{i}I don't know if you ever felt the same way about me. And I don't know what things will be like once you're gone.{/i}"

    "{i}Maybe we'll still talk every day. Maybe we'll slowly stop talking.{/i}"

    "{i}I really hope it's not the second one, haha.{/i}"

    "Your smile falters."

    "{i}But anyway...{/i}"

    "{i}I like you.{/i}"

    "{i}I've liked you for a while now, actually.{/i}"

    p "...What?"

    "You stare at the words."

    "For a moment, you wonder if you've read them wrong."

    "But the handwriting is unmistakable."

    "You continue reading."

    "{i}I don't know when it started. Maybe it was when you fell asleep during our study session and got mad at me for not waking you up.{/i}"

    "{i}Or when you bought me a drink because you remembered I didn't have enough money that day.{/i}"

    "{i}Or maybe there wasn't really a moment.{/i}"

    "{i}I just know that somewhere along the way, I started looking forward to seeing you more than I should have.{/i}"

    "{i}I wish I'd told you sooner.{/i}"

    "{i}And I really, really hate that you're leaving.{/i}"

    "{i}But I know you've been looking forward to living with your parents, too. So I can't exactly tell you not to go, can I?{/i}"

    "{i}I hope you'll be happy over there.{/i}"

    "{i}I hope you'll make lots of friends, eat good food, and have a life full of things that make you smile.{/i}"

    "{i}Even if we stop talking someday, I think knowing you're happy would make me happy too.{/i}"

    "{i}So take care of yourself, okay?{/i}"

    "{i}And don't forget about us too quickly.{/i}"

    "You lower the paper slightly."

    "Eight years."

    "For eight years, you'd thought you were the only one."

    "You'd spent so long convincing yourself that he only ever saw you as a friend."

    "All those moments you thought you'd imagined..."

    p "...You liked me?"

    "You look back down at the letter."

    "There's something else written at the bottom."

    "{i}P.S. You still owe me bubble tea, by the way. Don't think moving to another country gets you out of it.{/i}"

    "A small laugh escapes you."

    p "Seriously...?"

    "Your eyes sting."

    "{i}P.P.S. I was going to write something really cool here, but I can't think of anything.{/i}"

    "{i}...I miss you already.{/i}"

    "{i}And you haven't even left yet.{/i}"

    p "..."

    "You read those last two lines again."

    "And then once more."

    "For a long moment, you don't know what to say."

    "You laugh quietly, wiping at the corner of your eye."

    p "You idiot..."

    p "You could've just told me."

    "You pause."

    p "...Though I guess I wasn't any better."

    "You carefully fold the letter."

    "Then you look at the photograph again."

    "Five teenagers, smiling as though the world would stay exactly as it was."

    "You wonder what he looks like now."

    "Whether he still laughs the same way."

    "Whether he remembers those afternoons after school."

    p "I wonder where you are now..."

    "Your eyes drift back to his face in the photograph."

    "And suddenly, a memory comes rushing back."

    scene black
    with fade

    "A familiar voice."

    "The sound of footsteps outside Grandma's gate."

    "And someone shouting your name."

    jump chapter_1

    #Chapter 1 - The Boy Next Door

    label chapter_1:

        scene black
        with fade

        centered "CHAPTER 1"
        centered "The Boy Next Door"

        "Fourteen years ago."

        "Summer."

        "You were ten years old."

        "Back then, your world was very small."

        "There was the little house at the end of the street."

        "The convenience store where Grandma sometimes bought you ice cream."

        "The school that was only fifteen minutes away."

        "And a certain boy who never seemed to know when to shut up."

        b "OI!"

        b "ARE YOU AWAKE YET?"

        "..."

        b "HELLOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOOO?"

        p "..."

        p "Five more minutes..."

        b "WE'RE GONNA BE LATE!"

        p "Ugh... I'M COMING!"

        "You bury your face in your pillow."

        "It's too early."

        "And he's too loud."

        b "I'M COMING IN!"

        p "YOU BETTER NOT!"

        "A familiar laugh echoes from outside your window."

        b "THEN HURRY UP!"

        "You throw your blanket aside."

        p "I'm going to kill him."

        scene black
        with dissolve

        centered "KITCHEN"

        "By the time you stumble downstairs, he's already sitting at the kitchen table."

        "Of course he is."

        "He's halfway through a bowl of noodles."

        p "..."

        p "Why are you eating MY breakfast?"

        b "Grannie gave it to me."

        p "GRANDMA!!"

        gm "Don't shout so early in the morning, you two."

        "Grandma places another bowl on the table."

        gm "There's enough for both of you."

        b "See? Grannie likes me more."

